  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nFor Friday, deep upper low pressure will begin to move east towards \nthe Four Corners, with broad southwesterly flow developing aloft \nacross the Plains by Friday morning. At the surface, increasing lee \ncyclogenesis across eastern Colorado and Wyoming will lead to \nstrengthening south winds across the area by Friday afternoon. This \nis in response to diurnal mixing tapping into higher momentum flow \naloft, and promote wind gusts as high as 35 to 45 miles per hour. \nLuckily this will also coincide with increasing moisture advection, \nand this is expected to offset any greater fire weather concerns. \nThe aforementioned surface low pressure will begin to eject east \nacross the area Friday night, with an associated cold front pushing \nthrough the area from northwest to southeast by early Saturday \nafternoon. This front won't drift too far away from the area \nhowever, and should stall from northwest Kansas into portions of \neastern Nebraska by Saturday night. Some threat for precipitation \nmay arrive with this initial cold frontal passage, though NBM \nprobabilities of >0.10\" only increasing to as high as 10-20% across \nthe western Sandhills through early Saturday morning. \n\nThe greater threat for precipitation looks to arrive Saturday night \nand persist through the weekend and potentially into early next \nweek. By Saturday afternoon, the aforementioned surface front will be \npositioned from far southwest Nebraska to northeastern Nebraska, and \nwill begin to slowly lift back northwards into the area Sunday \nmorning. The broad moisture advection will push PWAT values above 1\" \nby Friday, and these will linger into Sunday night before a second \ncold front begins to push through by Monday morning. PWAT values \nthis high will approach the 99th percentile climo for late October, \nwith the most anomalous moisture along and ahead of the surface \nfrontal boundary. This is also where at least non-zero MUCAPE is \nexpected to overspread the area Sunday afternoon. Showers and \nembedded thunderstorms should increase in coverage by Sunday morning \nand persist through much of the day. Deterministic guidance remain \nin good agreement with respect to a swath of beneficial rain amounts \n(~0.5-1.5\") though still differ greatly on where exactly this \noccurs. This, combined with uncertainty on frontal boundary \nplacement, lends uncertainty to the exact location of heaviest \nrainfall accumulation across the area. As of now, NBM probabilities \nremain highest (~50-70% of >0.50\") from roughly Imperial to O'Neill \nand points to the east. Should any further eastward trend occur in \nthe placement of deeper moisture (due to a more progressive\nsurface front), the greater accumulations could also trend off \nto the east of the area. This will continue to be monitored \nclosely.\n\nSurface low pressure then begins to eject east across northern \nKansas late Sunday night, reaching eastern Nebraska Monday morning. \nThis drags a stronger cold front through the area by sunrise Monday, \nquickly ending any lingering precipitation from northwest to \nsoutheast. Guidance has begun to come into better agreement with \nthis quick cessation of lingering precipitation, leading to \nincreasing confidence in no mixed or wintry precipitation Monday \nmorning. This brings a return of much cooler highs as well, with \ntemperatures struggling to leave the upper 40s to low 50s Monday \nafternoon. Zonal flow briefly returns aloft Tuesday, before a \nnorthern stream trough begins to cross the northern Plains into \nWednesday. This will lead to slightly moderating temperatures and \nbriefly drier conditions, with highs moderating back to the lower \n60s for Wednesday.",
